<<<
prefix (Gram.); ‘attached in front’. An affix (see above) attached to the beginningof a word orstem to makea f compound word, as re-, ex-, be-, a-, in reform, ex-officer, belabour, arise.
===
*prefix* (Gram.); 'attached in front'. An *affix* (see above) attached to the beginning of a word or stem to make a compound word, as re-, ex-, be-, a-, in *reform, ex-officer, belabour, arise*.
>>>

<<<
pregnant construction (Gram.). ‘But Philip was found at Azotus’ is in the Greek ‘ But Philip was found to Azotus’; i. e., the expressed sentence contains an implied one— Philip was conveyed to & Philip was found at Azotus.
===
*pregnant construction* (Gram.). 'But Philip was found *at* Azotus' is in the Greek 'But Philip was found *to* Azotus'; i.e., the expressed sentence contains an implied one—Philip was conveyed to & Philip was found at Azotus.
>>>

<<<
premise (Log.); ‘thing sent before’. See syllogism below. rivative (Gram.); ‘ taking away’. |) p g ya Prefixes that deny the presence of the quality denoted by the simple ff word are called p. or negative.
===
*premise* (Log.); 'thing sent before'. See *syllogism* below.

*privative* (Gram.); 'taking away'. Prefixes that deny the presence of the quality denoted by the simple word are called *p.* or *negative*.
>>>

<<<
The |i a- of aseptic & the in- of innocent [i are privative, whereas the a- of arise & the in- of insist are not.
===
The a- of *aseptic* & the in- of *innocent* are privative, whereas the a- of *arise* & the in- of *insist* are not.
>>>

